## From the springs to a spa town

The mineral springs around today's Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) had been known for centuries to the canons of the nearby Premonstratensian monastery of Teplá, founded in 1193 by the nobleman Hroznata. The Cross Spring is first mentioned in the monastery annals in 1749 as a "salty acidulous water"; the same year a wooden cross was erected beside it, from which it took its name. At the close of the 18th century the monastery physician Josef Jan Nehr took up the systematic study of the springs, and in 1805–1807 he built the first brick house for spa guests by the Cross Spring. His work helped persuade the Teplá monastery to begin developing the site for spa guests.

The place has borne the name Mariánské Lázně since 1808. In 1812, by decision of Abbot Karl Kaspar Reitenberger, it became an independent municipality, and on 6 November 1818 Mariánské Lázně was declared a public spa. It became a town on 29 May 1865 by decision of Emperor Franz Joseph I, and received its coat of arms on 1 June 1866. In the course of the 19th century the settlement by the springs thus grew into a town of spa houses, hotels, colonnades and extensive parks.

## The flowering of the 19th century

The new spa quickly won an international reputation. Guests came for the drinking cure, the baths and the social life. After the founding neoclassical phase of 1817–1823 came the town's golden age of 1870–1914, when a large part of the buildings that define the town to this day were built: the Church of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary (1844–1848), the Casino assembly rooms (1867), the Municipal Theatre (1868), the Main Colonnade (1888–1889), Nové Lázně (1896) and the hotels along Hlavní třída. The railway connected the town with Cheb, Plzeň, Prague and Vienna in 1872.

The town attracted monarchs, artists, scientists and politicians. Goethe visited Mariánské Lázně three times between 1820 and 1823, Chopin came in 1836, Mark Twain in the summer of 1891, and the British King Edward VII came nine times between 1897 and 1909. In his presence, on 21 August 1905, the course of the golf club founded in 1904 was ceremonially opened. More detailed stories are on the [Famous Visitors](/en/famous-visitors) page.

