## A town of parks and colonnades

The shape of the spa centre was determined not only by buildings. From 1817 the landscape gardener Václav Skalník began transforming the damp valley into an open park. Promenades linked the hotels with the springs and created a setting in which treatment was naturally complemented by walking and time spent outdoors.

The Main Colonnade of 1888–1889, 135 metres long, became the town's best-known symbol. Nearby stands the Cross Spring pavilion of 1818–1826, and in front of the colonnade the Singing Fountain has played since 1986.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/colonnade/colonnade-interior-hall.jpg"
