   caption="The Main Colonnade combines spa architecture with the everyday promenade."
   width="wide" /%}

## Mariánské Lázně in the 20th century

In the 1920s and 1930s spa life continued; guests included T. G. Masaryk and Edvard Beneš. Both world wars interrupted international spa operations, and during the Second World War Mariánské Lázně was a hospital town. Post-war changes in population and in the ownership of the spa houses transformed the character of the town; spa care focused chiefly on patients of the Czechoslovak health service.

After 1989 the restoration of the historic buildings began, along with the gradual return of foreign guests. Throughout, the town has remained a living spa centre, not merely a historic monument.

## UNESCO and the present

In July 2021 Mariánské Lázně, together with ten other European towns, was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List as part of the property **The Great Spa Towns of Europe**. The inscription honours the interplay of springs, spa architecture, parks and traditions that shaped European spa culture.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/town/aerial-central-spa-building.jpg"
   alt="Aerial view of the Centrální lázně spa building with its domed tower below a wooded slope"
   caption="The historic centre rises between the spa parks and the wooded slopes."
   width="wide" /%}

In 2023 the healing climate of Mariánské Lázně was officially confirmed, and the town uses the designation of a climatic spa. Alongside the mineral springs, spring gas and peat, all four of its natural healing resources are thus professionally confirmed.

## What you can visit from its history

- **The Municipal Museum** is housed in the Golden Grape House of 1818, where Goethe stayed in 1823.
- **The Main Colonnade** recalls the town's flowering at the end of the 19th century.
- **Nové Lázně** of 1896 preserves the historic Roman Baths and the Royal Cabin.
- **The Casino assembly rooms** serve concerts, balls and social events.
- **The Municipal Theatre** offers a programme in its own historic building of 1868.

